ection northwest: G7 (row G, col7) → F6 (row F, col6), E5 (row E, col5), D4 (row D, col4).\n\nF6 is *, E5 is *, D4 is @.\n\nSo yes, sandwich. So placing at G7 would flip F6 and E5.\n\nThat\'s 2 pieces.\n\nOther directions?\n\nEast: G8 empty.\n\nWest: G6 empty.\n\nSouth: H7 empty.\n\nNortheast: F8 empty.\n\nSouthwest: H6 empty.\n\nNorth: column7: G7, F7 (*), E7 empty. So no.\n\nSoutheast: H8 empty.\n\nSo only northwest direction gives 2 flips.\n\nOkay, so summarizing the possible captures:\n\n- (A,6): 1 flip (B5)\n- (B,4): 1 flip (C5)\n- (B,6): 1 flip (C5)\n- (C,6): 2 flips (C5, D5)\n- (E,6): 2 flips (E5, D5)\n- (F,4): 1 flip (E5)\n- (G,6): 1 flip (F5)\n- (G,7): 2 flips (F6, E5)\n\nSo the moves with 2 flips are C6, E6, G7.\n\nNow, need to evaluate which of these is better.\n\nIn Reversi, capturing more pieces is good, but also strategic placement matters. Corners are very valuable, edges less so, and you want to avoid moves that allow the opponent to take corners.\n\nLooking at the current board:\n\nThe current @ pieces are concentrated on the left side (columns 1-5), especially around the center. The opponent has a solid line in column 5 from B5 to F5.\n\nLet\'s look at each of the 2-flip moves.\n\nC6: Flips C5 and D5. This would convert two *s in column5 to @s. Column5 currently has B5*, C5*, D5*, E5*, F5*. After flipping C5 and D5, column5 would have B5*, C5@, D5@, E5*, F5*. So it weakens the opponent\'s column5 line.\n\nE6: Flips E5 and D5. Similarly, converts E5 and D5 to @. Column5 would have B5*, C5*, D5@, E5@, F5*.\n\nG7: Flips F6 and E5. F6 is in column6, which currently has D6@, E6 empty, F6*, G6 empty. Flipping F6 would add another @ there. Also flipping E5.\n\nBut G7 is near the edge (column7), but not a corner. However, placing at G7 might be risky if it allows opponent to take H8 or something.\n\nBut let\'s think about each move\'s strategic value.\n\nC6: This is in row C, column6. Flipping C5 and D5. This move is on the upper part of the board. It migh