ad to more long-term gain.\n\nWait, but let's check if A3 is a safe move. After flipping those pieces, are there any weaknesses created?\n\nFor example, after flipping C5 to @, what about the D6 area? D6 is @, so maybe that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the opponent can respond by taking A2, which would then sandwich A1 and A3? Wait, no: if opponent takes A2, they need to have a line where their new piece sandwiches my pieces. If A2 is *, then looking east: A3 is @, A4 is @. So no sandwich. Looking south: B2 is @, so no. So opponent couldn't sandwich anything if they took A2. So A2 would not be a valid move for them after I take A3. Wait, but A2 is empty now. After I take A3, A2 is still empty. Could opponent play A2?\n\nLet's see: opponent's turn after I play A3.\n\nBoard after A3:\n\nA row: @ . @ @ @ @ @ @\n\nSo A2 is still empty.\n\nOpponent wants to play A2. To do so, they need to have a direction where from A2, there's a * with all between being @s.\n\nFor example, east direction: A3 is @, A4 is @. So no. South direction: B2 is @. So no. Southeast: B3 is now @ (after flip), so no. So opponent cannot sandwich anything if they play A2. Therefore, A2 would not be a valid move for them. So taking A3 makes A2 still invalid for opponent.\n\nTherefore, A3 seems safe.\n\nAnother point: the move A3 is on the edge (row A), which is generally less desirable than inner moves, but since it's connecting to existing pieces and flipping multiple, it's okay.\n\nComparing to H1: taking the corner. But let's see the board after H1.\n\nAfter H1:\n\nH1 becomes @.\n\nFlips G1 and G2 to @.\n\nG row was * * * @ @ @ * *, so after flipping G1 and G2, G1 and G2 become @.\n\nSo G row becomes @ @ * @ @ @ * *\n\nThis strengthens the G row, but H1 is a corner.\n\nHowever, the opponent may then take H3 (which is empty), or other squares.\n\nBut the main point is whether securing the corner outweighs the four flips.\n\nIn general, corners are very valuable, but four flips in the center/left